instruction:
You are working in the dayjs repository checked out at /dayjs (a JavaScript date library, jest tests, node_modules already installed). Do NOT touch anything outside /dayjs. Do not use the internet.

GOAL: (A) establish the baseline test status, (B) apply one specific source fix, (C) verify.

STEP A - BASELINE (before any edit):
Run these commands from /dayjs and save the output to /tmp/baseline.txt :
  cd /dayjs && npx jest --coverage=false 2>&1 | tail -80
  cd /dayjs && npx jest test/plugin/timezone.test.js --coverage=false 2>&1 | tail -60
  cd /dayjs && TZ=Europe/London npx jest test/plugin/timezone.test.js --coverage=false 2>&1 | tail -60
  cd /dayjs && TZ=Pacific/Auckland npx jest test/plugin/timezone.test.js --coverage=false 2>&1 | tail -60
Report exactly which test suites/tests FAIL at baseline (names + counts).

STEP B - THE FIX:
Edit the file /dayjs/src/plugin/timezone/index.js. The function `d.tz` currently reads:

  d.tz = function (input, timezone) {
    const previousOffset = tzOffset(+d(), timezone)
    const localTs = d.utc(input).valueOf()
    const [targetTs, targetOffset] = fixOffset(localTs, previousOffset, timezone)
    const ins = d(targetTs).utcOffset(targetOffset)
    return ins
  }

Change it so that non-string inputs (a numeric timestamp, a JS Date, or a Day.js object) are treated as an instant in the local time zone and converted with the instance `tz` method, instead of being run through `d.utc(input)`. The new body must be exactly:

  d.tz = function (input, timezone) {
    const previousOffset = tzOffset(+d(), timezone)
    if (typeof input !== 'string') {
      // timestamp, js Date, Day.js object
      return d(input).tz(timezone)
    }
    const localTs = d.utc(input).valueOf()
    const [targetTs, targetOffset] = fixOffset(localTs, previousOffset, timezone)
    const ins = d(targetTs).utcOffset(targetOffset)
    return ins
  }

Make ONLY this change. Do not reformat or change anything else in the file. Do not modify any test file.

STEP C - VERIFY:
1. Re-run the same four jest commands and diff against baseline. Report any test that was passing before and fails after (this must be none).
2. Additionally write a temporary scratch script at /tmp/check.js (OUTSIDE the repo, do not add files to /dayjs) that uses babel-node or jest to check the new behaviour. The easiest reliable way: create a temporary test file OUTSIDE the repo is not possible because jest roots = test/. Instead run this with node + @babel/register or simply use `cd /dayjs && npx babel-node` if available; if not, use `npx jest` with a temporary file /dayjs/test/tmp_check.test.js, run it, then DELETE it afterwards so the repo is left clean (verify with `git status --porcelain` that only src/plugin/timezone/index.js is modified).
   The check must verify, for timezone 'America/New_York':
     - dayjs.tz(1401638400000, 'America/New_York').format() === moment.tz(1401638400000, 'America/New_York').format()
     - dayjs.tz(new Date(1401638400000), 'America/New_York').format() === moment.tz(1401638400000, 'America/New_York').format()
     - dayjs.tz(dayjs(1401638400000), 'America/New_York').format() === moment.tz(1401638400000, 'America/New_York').format()
     - valueOf() and utcOffset() of each match moment's
     - the string-input behaviour is unchanged: dayjs.tz('2014-06-01 12:00', 'America/New_York').format() === '2014-06-01T12:00:00-04:00'
   Run that check under at least TZ=UTC and TZ=Europe/London and TZ=Pacific/Auckland (e.g. `TZ=Europe/London npx jest test/tmp_check.test.js --coverage=false`).
3. Run `cd /dayjs && npx eslint src/plugin/timezone/index.js` and report the result.

FINAL REPORT: report (i) baseline failing tests, (ii) the exact diff applied (git diff), (iii) post-fix failing tests, (iv) the results of the scratch checks under each TZ, (v) confirmation that `git status --porcelain` shows only src/plugin/timezone/index.js modified, and (vi) any test that regressed.

Repo: /dayjs (dayjs JS date library, jest, node_modules installed). Work ONLY inside /dayjs. No internet. Another subagent is concurrently editing src/plugin/timezone/index.js and another src/plugin/customParseFormat/index.js and another src/locale/nl.js — you must NOT touch those files. You may create/delete your own temp test file /dayjs/test/zz_tmp_weekyear.test.js.

BUG TO FIX (src/plugin/weekYear/index.js): the plugin currently reads:

export default (o, c) => {
  const proto = c.prototype
  proto.weekYear = function () {
    const month = this.month()
    const weekOfYear = this.week()
    const year = this.year()
    if (weekOfYear === 1 && month === 11) {
      return year + 1
    }
    return year
  }
}

It is missing the symmetric case: a date in early January that belongs to the last week of the PREVIOUS week-year (e.g. 2016-01-01 with the 'en-gb' locale, which has yearStart: 4, is ISO week 53 of week-year 2015). Add exactly this branch after the existing `if (weekOfYear === 1 && month === 11)` block and before `return year`:

    if (month === 0 && weekOfYear >= 52) {
      return year - 1
    }

Make ONLY that change (keep the file's existing style/indentation). Do not modify any other file.

VERIFY:
1. Write /dayjs/test/zz_tmp_weekyear.test.js that, for each of the locales 'en' and 'en-gb' (use dayjs.locale(name) and moment.locale(name); 'en-gb' is at ../src/locale/en-gb and must be imported for dayjs), compares dayjs(date).weekYear() with moment(date).weekYear() for every day from 2010-01-01 to 2025-12-31 (generate with a loop, format 'YYYY-MM-DD'). Print every mismatch as `locale | date | dayjs=.. | moment=..` and also print the total number of mismatches. Run it with: cd /dayjs && npx jest test/zz_tmp_weekyear.test.js --coverage=false 2>&1 | head -100
2. Also run the repo's own weekYear/weekOfYear tests: cd /dayjs && npx jest test/plugin/weekYear.test.js test/plugin/weekOfYear.test.js --coverage=false
3. Run the FULL suite before and after your edit (cd /dayjs && npx jest --coverage=false 2>&1 | tail -20) and report any test that passed before and fails after. Baseline for this container (TZ=UTC) is: 56 suites, 524 tests, all passing.
4. DELETE /dayjs/test/zz_tmp_weekyear.test.js afterwards and confirm `git status --porcelain` shows only src/plugin/weekYear/index.js modified by you.

FINAL REPORT: the exact diff, the mismatch counts before and after the fix for both locales, the full-suite result before/after, and confirmation the temp file was deleted.

Repo: /dayjs (dayjs JS date library, jest, node_modules installed). Work ONLY inside /dayjs. No internet. Other subagents are concurrently editing src/plugin/timezone/index.js, src/plugin/weekYear/index.js and src/locale/nl.js — do NOT touch those files. You may create/delete your own temp test file /dayjs/test/zz_tmp_meridiem.test.js.

BUG TO FIX (src/plugin/customParseFormat/index.js): the `A` and `a` format tokens only understand hard-coded English AM/PM and ignore the locale's `meridiem` function, so parsing a meridiem string of a locale that defines `meridiem` (e.g. the Chinese locales returning '上午'/'下午') fails. The file currently has:

const matchUpperCaseAMPM = /[AP]M/
const matchLowerCaseAMPM = /[ap]m/
...
  A: [matchUpperCaseAMPM, function (input) {
    this.afternoon = input === 'PM'
  }],
  a: [matchLowerCaseAMPM, function (input) {
    this.afternoon = input === 'pm'
  }],

Apply exactly this fix:
(a) DELETE the two lines defining `matchUpperCaseAMPM` and `matchLowerCaseAMPM`.
(b) Add this helper function immediately BEFORE the `const expressions = {` object (it must be after `const getLocalePart = ...` so that the module-level `locale` variable is in scope):

const meridiemMatch = (input, isLowerCase) => {
  let isAfternoon
  const { meridiem } = locale
  if (!meridiem) {
    isAfternoon = input === (isLowerCase ? 'pm' : 'PM')
  } else {
    for (let i = 1; i <= 24; i += 1) {
      // todo: fix input === meridiem(i, 0, isLowerCase)
      if (input.indexOf(meridiem(i, 0, isLowerCase)) > -1) {
        isAfternoon = i > 12
        break
      }
    }
  }
  return isAfternoon
}

(c) Replace the A and a entries of `expressions` with:

  A: [matchWord, function (input) {
    this.afternoon = meridiemMatch(input, false)
  }],
  a: [matchWord, function (input) {
    this.afternoon = meridiemMatch(input, true)
  }],

(`matchWord` is already defined in the file as /\d*[^\s\d-:/()]+/ .) Change nothing else.

VERIFY:
1. Write /dayjs/test/zz_tmp_meridiem.test.js. It must (i) re-run the behaviour of the existing meridiem tests (parsing 'AM'/'PM' and 'am'/'pm' with the default locale and with an explicit locale), and (ii) test a custom locale that defines `meridiem`, e.g.
   const locale = { name: 'zz-meridiem', weekdays: 'aaa_bbb_ccc_ddd_eee_fff_ggg'.split('_'), months: 'AAA_BBB_CCC_DDD_EEE_FFF_GGG_HHH_III_JJJ_KKK_LLL_MMM'.split('_'), meridiem: (hour, minute, isLowercase) => { const hm = hour * 100 + minute; if (hm < 600) return '凌晨'; if (hm < 900) return '早上'; if (hm < 1130) return '上午'; if (hm < 1230) return '中午'; if (hm < 1800) return '下午'; return '晚上' } }
   and check that dayjs('2018-01-01 上午 12:00', 'YYYY-MM-DD A hh:mm', locale).format('YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm') === '2018-01-01 00:00' and dayjs('2018-01-01 下午 12:00', 'YYYY-MM-DD A hh:mm', locale).format('YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm') === '2018-01-01 12:00', plus the same with lowercase meridiem and the 'a' token. Print all actual values.
2. Run: cd /dayjs && npx jest test/plugin/customParseFormat.test.js --coverage=false 2>&1 | tail -40 and make sure every pre-existing test in that file still passes (especially the ones using the format 'D.M.YY H:m:s.S A ZZ' and 'correctly parse ordinal').
3. Run the FULL suite before and after your edit (cd /dayjs && npx jest --coverage=false 2>&1 | tail -20). Baseline in this container (TZ=UTC) is 56 suites / 524 tests all passing; report any test that passed before and fails after.
4. DELETE /dayjs/test/zz_tmp_meridiem.test.js afterwards and confirm `git status --porcelain` shows only src/plugin/customParseFormat/index.js modified by you.

FINAL REPORT: the exact diff, the results of the custom-meridiem checks, the customParseFormat suite result, the full-suite result before/after, and confirmation the temp file was deleted.

